son} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083447}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436918} :activity/id #uuid "1f7d750a-49b8-5dec-8954-584295b47047" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050429 :topic/id #uuid "486a09f3-45ae-5574-b216-0a329beb1dea" :topic/math-academy-id 757} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34bfc3ba-449c-528d-88b0-6f1a8ead8ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348923} {:answer/id #uuid "af6b758e-a4f3-5184-8f82-847476a988d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349996} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c900ac-0efe-5fde-9ee8-9f8185818850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350007} {:answer/id #uuid "b791b1f1-6e46-536c-99de-2068be805c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350072} {:answer/id #uuid "f76f16cd-5491-5f45-a011-0e4b32a84811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350007} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbdb52c0-0282-5bdb-8a53-fe9c6f8974e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d24742b9-e0e3-539d-a243-8418f31d72e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83815" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "58e58414-042e-556c-946f-9319d6217a9e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273c6dd0-67fe-528d-98c1-8dad19a58e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436911} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0177fe-6f1f-5c81-84de-6277949370ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436912} {:answer/id #uuid "8a571250-310b-5aff-b6f3-4de1c0dc69f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436913} {:answer/id #uuid "ad17155f-b4cd-54ec-8be4-28ea64a81659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436914} {:answer/id #uuid "b2275ff8-fe98-5084-bd82-ea5aaba7e949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436912} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eafbdc9-1f3b-58eb-b092-b6276d35ea08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "66ae1650-566a-5bce-8d4c-8b63d98b0a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-14140" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1\\,\\pm \\frac{6}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{12}{5},\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $24$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 12=24.$)"} :step/id #uuid "74fc6fe2-50c7-5458-827e-99d2e1df0a79" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135f42a4-d1ee-5f2c-af3a-0c8aa0176bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324143} {:answer/id #uuid "268df909-c8fb-563f-af2f-c08134584f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324283} {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6363-b72d-5a0d-9eec-918fa9888a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325152} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9d3f67-6d9d-5067-8456-4fe529f4d545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2953d1-fb0d-5e3e-8ec9-21bd1672c52d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ff2104b-6a5b-5774-95cc-77db99183716" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436917}}] :activity/title "The Rational Roots Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436910}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492961} :activity/id #uuid "23e0df44-7023-5786-bcc6-bf2a24b2da0f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050429 :topic/id #uuid "486a09f3-45ae-5574-b216-0a329beb1dea" :topic/math-academy-id 757} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09623bc9-a857-55e4-9eb6-62ab35d1cac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299980} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9e6e03-d8f7-519b-bf8f-09b6838f4775" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7782f6dc-5bfd-598b-a7bf-b3050e3b4fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-323215" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=16{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+7x+12$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 6,$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 8,$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\  & \\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $28$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 14=28.$)"} :step/id #uuid "1c78b6e5-0b3d-542f-981c-72db0b806463" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502dce7e-39fd-5d3e-90fe-883c9d4faed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492370} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5435d7-226a-5cc7-911d-fda5e505d743" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7649552c-e2dd-537f-80f0-06e90c2af71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-323223" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+3$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 3.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2,$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"} :step/id #uuid "3d4f4a1a-0d09-50c6-9d90-b5ac235d5b30" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433636} {:answer/id #uuid "7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433647} {:answer/id #uuid "8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433649} {:answer/id #uuid "d26b2ae3-d848-5cfd-a798-719a96527d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433659} {:answer/id #uuid "e42f90f8-5654-57c3-8684-e319bb32b08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433649} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-83824" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$"} :step/id #uuid "49d109c1-966d-5d06-a1b4-37f9036c2497" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3916b332-b1bf-56dd-ba24-217edc46155a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333522} {:answer/id #uuid "69ad4502-e492-59c7-a566-d06801cfd838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955} {:answer/id #uuid "75a12632-6342-5324-a3b6-e62706c87939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334051} {:answer/id #uuid "786075ed-ee83-5fb6-a116-f1eab63b1476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334069} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb95b3c-af6d-5fd3-b35b-7b81469d3cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955} :answer-field/id #uuid "6502c1b8-0bfa-5633-9f9c-b5117737a077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d02cc2f-0a59-5f12-841d-8b0228cd0395" :question/math-academy-id "q-6657" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-1}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "7bbb91cb-0115-59be-b84d-383083ba59ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17d593-2ae4-53d7-a4b5-82e61d1bfa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ef24a4-cbc2-5e7b-841f-6bdc135e149a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12d33f18-863a-5b25-ace7-f97482164c70" :question/math-academy-id "q-323572" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x-15$ has a root in the interval $(1,4),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }15}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 15.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(1,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $15$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+2x+5).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x+5$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =4-20 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "82d25e83-31f3-50b5-8226-00ac5920f5f0"}] :activity/title "The Rational Roots Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492205}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509013} :activity/id #uuid "ce408938-6ff2-5ee9-9af3-b3e6dc563bd5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050429 :topic/id #uuid "486a09f3-45ae-5574-b216-0a329beb1dea" :topic/math-academy-id 757} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c017070-88fd-5d41-924a-dd54f0531459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244703} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac6a7f4-b7e5-5aab-882f-f2ec82413511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3708f5-187e-5b74-bb7c-fb6773b51adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245410} {:answer/id #uuid "b691b5c9-8cce-5aa4-9d4c-8759d0a10381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245946} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2dabbb-0466-5a5e-8205-efb8aaaa4a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0be498-1922-5ad5-b70c-3159737e4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6b933a9-c0e8-5275-ae25-ac03d96707b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-112875" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "5496c088-fa95-5052-8432-a8ee6731ca57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000d0b8b-482a-53a8-87ce-fe362b914282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508388} {:answer/id #uuid "3334f6ee-ea48-5b27-93c8-f3725a806b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508466} {:answer/id #uuid "50c09e5c-1c9d-53b7-9773-98ff2805aa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508510} {:answer/id #uuid "67e31522-060a-5e95-9560-1ac9b15d8ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508543} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e893b0-43f9-5fae-8bb4-0d4cfd2cce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508388} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68114ce-13fb-5bcc-bd0f-4d1c50824307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87021664-1460-517e-b0da-ae45fc0bbb9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83831" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+x+1,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)"} :step/id #uuid "58d4a216-d386-5004-acc6-1259a07e1494" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b16f84b-0fdd-5a3e-8aa0-dc62596d443c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300664} :answer-field/id #uuid "410552d6-05ce-5fcd-88f6-0b3a368b5a06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fd8abfc9-fc42-5fbe-8914-ffe40a38eb8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-323218" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)"} :step/id #uuid "98570077-079e-5420-bc17-984e3fb96b3b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c0af0-b447-58fd-8761-71af3c0f95df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea9a655-49ca-533c-8cb7-421e176fbf73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060cda53-8668-5be5-8aef-25b100d05754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e37fe1-bd7c-51e8-ba48-a531ae7460a1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b6ea38-f33a-5329-8a75-bcbcd85f7f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f8d121-1dd4-5d54-b226-07216b17964f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3518da80-0db9-569e-b980-2b5efc57c5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-323819" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c5006e3f-438a-526e-aab3-e8ec1d08a5ad"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17d593-2ae4-53d7-a4b5-82e61d1bfa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ef24a4-cbc2-5e7b-841f-6bdc135e149a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12d33f18-863a-5b25-ace7-f97482164c70" :question/math-academy-id "q-323572" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x-15$ has a root in the interval $(1,4),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }15}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 15.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(1,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $15$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+2x+5).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x+5$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =4-20 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "d86879e5-509b-538b-b3ff-c28fdb97936e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509036}}] :activity/title "The Rational Roots Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508386}]}
